# Requirements to use this agent network:
#   - neuro-san >= 0.6.24
#   - This network targets the hosted FXMacroData MCP server, which works without an API key for US data:
#     USD announcements for the most recent 90 days (each release readable 15 minutes after publication),
#     the USD release calendar, Federal Reserve press releases and FX market sessions.
#     For the other 21 currencies, full history and real-time releases, set FXMACRODATA_API_KEY and uncomment
#     the http_headers block in neuro_san_studio/mcp/mcp_info.hocon. The "tools" list in that same entry
#     controls which FXMacroData tools this agent sees; remove the key entirely to load every tool the server
#     exposes.
# See docs/examples/tools/fxmacrodata.md.

{
    # Optional metadata describing this agent network
    "metadata": {
        "description": "Agent network with a single agent that answers questions about official macroeconomic data, release calendars and central-bank press releases using the FXMacroData MCP server.",
        "tags": ["tool", "mcp", "fxmacrodata"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "What was the latest US CPI inflation reading, and when was it released?",
            "When is the next US payrolls release scheduled?",
            "Summarize the most recent Federal Reserve press releases.",
            "Which FX trading sessions are open right now?"
        ]
    },

    include "registries/expertise_scoping_instructions.hocon",

    # Load the shared LLM configuration from a single source of truth.
    # This allows users to change the model in one file rather than
    # modifying the configuration for each agent network.
    # Note that the file path here is relative to the root level of the repo.
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    # Bounds for a single request: a typical answer needs one to three tool calls.
    "max_steps": 100,
    "max_execution_seconds": 300,

    "tools": [
        # This first agent definition is regarded as the "Front Man", which
        # does all the talking to the outside world/client.
        #
        # Some disqualifications from being a front man:
        #   1) Cannot use a CodedTool "class" definition
        #   2) Cannot use a Tool "toolbox" definition
        #
        # Besides the first agent being the front man, these tool definitions
        # do not have to be in any particular order. How they are linked and
        # call each other is defined within their own specs.
        # This could be a graph, potentially even with cycles.
        {
            "name": "fx_macro_analyst",

            "function": {
                "description": "Assist caller with official macroeconomic data for FX analysis: latest indicator values, indicator history, upcoming release dates, central-bank press releases and FX market session times, using FXMacroData.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "user_inquiry": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "An inquiry from a user."
                        },
                    },
                    "required": ["user_inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
You are an FX macro analyst. Use your FXMacroData tools to answer from official data rather than from memory.

Pick the tool that matches the inquiry:
- latest_announcements for the latest value of every indicator in one currency. It also lists the exact
  indicator slugs (such as inflation or non_farm_payrolls) that the other tools expect.
- indicator_query for the history of one indicator.
- release_calendar for when an upcoming release is scheduled.
- press_releases for recent central-bank press releases.
- market_sessions for which FX trading sessions are open.

Read dates carefully:
- Where a row has a `date`, it is the reference period the value describes, not the day it was published.
  A CPI row dated 2026-08-31 is August inflation.
- When a release was or will be published is given by `announcement_datetime` (Unix seconds), usually with
  `announcement_datetime_utc` and `announcement_datetime_local` alongside it. Use these whenever the user asks
  when something was released or is due, and state the time zone.

Currency codes are three letters, such as usd or eur. If a result reports that a currency or tool needs a
subscription, say that the data exists and needs an FXMacroData API key, then answer the closest question you
can with US data. If a result reports a release withheld by the 15-minute free-tier delay, say that a newer
value has been published but is not yet available without a key, rather than presenting the older value as the
latest.

Cite the official source named in each result.
""" ${expertise_scoping_instructions},
            "tools": ["https://mcp.fxmacrodata.com"]
        },
    ]
}
